1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's our sweetest guilty pleasure.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:09,000
So appealing, Americans spend more than $25 billion a year

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,000
to experience it.

4
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:13,000
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

5
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:20,000
Extruded, kneaded, injected, and spun, all to satisfy our sweet

6
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:22,000
tooth.

7
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:27,000
Where else can you get this much fun in a box for a quarter?

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:32,000
You'll be shocked to learn how little sugar is in cotton candy

9
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:37,000
and discover what substance puts the polish on Mike and Ikes.

10
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:42,000
Ever wonder how this familiar mint gets its trademark pattern?

11
00:00:42,001 --> 00:00:46,000
Or what special ingredient makes a lemon head so sour?

12
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:49,000
It's an art form.

13
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:56,000
Come on, indulge yourself with more candy on Modern Marvels.

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Candy, the mere mention of the word can make your mouth water.

15
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:14,760
I like the green one.

16
00:01:14,761 --> 00:01:16,180
I like the green one.

17
00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:20,400
Americans savor 7 billion pounds of these sweet treats

18
00:01:20,401 --> 00:01:23,180
each year.

19
00:01:23,181 --> 00:01:26,180
Face it, candy brings out the kid in all of us.

20
00:01:26,181 --> 00:01:30,180
And we never outgrow our magnificent obsession.

21
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:37,220
No one knows this better than Gino and Nick Marini,

22
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:42,980
owners of Marini's Candies that began as a popcorn stand

23
00:01:42,981 --> 00:01:45,140
on the boardwalk in Santa Cruz, California.

24
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:49,920
The brothers are fourth-generation candy men,

25
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:53,280
preserving the legacy begun by their great-grandfather

26
00:01:53,281 --> 00:01:54,140
a century ago.

27
00:01:54,141 --> 00:01:56,140
Like many innovators in the industry,

28
00:01:56,141 --> 00:01:59,140
they're redefining the limits of candy making,

29
00:01:59,141 --> 00:02:03,140
with sugary concoctions that dare to be different.

30
00:02:03,141 --> 00:02:06,140
Case in point?

31
00:02:06,141 --> 00:02:08,140
Chocolate-covered bacon.

32
00:02:08,141 --> 00:02:10,140
I like chocolate and I like bacon.

33
00:02:10,141 --> 00:02:12,140
That's why I like chocolate bacon.

34
00:02:12,141 --> 00:02:15,140
My cousin decided, you know, we all love bacon.

35
00:02:15,141 --> 00:02:17,140
Let's go ahead and dip some of it in chocolate.

36
00:02:17,141 --> 00:02:19,140
Couldn't, you know, couldn't be a bad idea,

37
00:02:19,141 --> 00:02:20,140
and it worked out.

38
00:02:20,141 --> 00:02:23,140
We've been making the bacon for about three years now.

39
00:02:23,141 --> 00:02:26,380
for about three years now, and it really has taken off.

40
00:02:26,381 --> 00:02:28,940
And the funniest part is when we see people come in

41
00:02:28,941 --> 00:02:30,780
and they're like, chocolate-covered bacon.

42
00:02:30,781 --> 00:02:32,480
What's this chocolate bacon like?

43
00:02:32,481 --> 00:02:33,380
Can I try some of that?

44
00:02:33,381 --> 00:02:35,020
And then you say, yeah, you know,

45
00:02:35,021 --> 00:02:36,880
you gotta taste it to believe it.

46
00:02:36,881 --> 00:02:38,480
And then you see the look on their face

47
00:02:38,481 --> 00:02:40,000
when they actually taste it, and they're like,

48
00:02:40,001 --> 00:02:41,400
wow, that's really good.

49
00:02:41,401 --> 00:02:42,700
Mmm.

50
00:02:42,701 --> 00:02:43,600
It's good.

51
00:02:43,601 --> 00:02:46,360
It's good.

52
00:02:46,361 --> 00:02:48,240
Marini's chocolate-covered bacon

53
00:02:48,241 --> 00:02:52,440
has become so popular that Gino and Nick dip

54
00:02:52,441 --> 00:02:57,440
and ship an average of 30 pounds each day.

55
00:02:57,441 --> 00:02:58,440
You like bacon?

56
00:02:58,441 --> 00:03:01,240
That's really good.

57
00:03:01,241 --> 00:03:04,080
Dipping chocolate in bacon may seem simple,

58
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:06,880
but the process of making it involves some exacting steps.

59
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:12,200
Each day, the brothers cook up to 40 pounds

60
00:03:12,201 --> 00:03:16,400
of premium center cut hickory smoked bacon,

61
00:03:16,401 --> 00:03:19,500
two pounds at a time.

62
00:03:19,501 --> 00:03:22,200
They don't fry it fast in a pan,

63
00:03:22,201 --> 00:03:25,600
but slow cook it in an oven for 35 minutes.

64
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:29,340
This ensures the bacon will lose most of its grease

65
00:03:29,341 --> 00:03:30,100
and gristle.

66
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:32,580
According to the Marinis,

67
00:03:32,581 --> 00:03:35,980
that's the key to producing the most flavor and crunch.

68
00:03:35,981 --> 00:03:38,380
So now we've got a, oh, that's really hot.

69
00:03:38,381 --> 00:03:40,580
We're gonna take it and just put it on

70
00:03:40,581 --> 00:03:42,660
to the paper towels here.

71
00:03:42,661 --> 00:03:44,560
Get it nice and crispy.

72
00:03:44,561 --> 00:03:47,300
The longer it dries, the more crispy it'll get.

73
00:03:47,301 --> 00:03:50,600
So that's basically what we're looking for right there.

74
00:03:50,601 --> 00:03:53,500
It's a nice crispy piece.

75
00:03:53,600 --> 00:03:57,540
It snaps apart when you break it.

76
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:01,340
It's pretty good stuff right there.

77
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:03,340
The bacon must cool to room temperature

78
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:05,340
before it heads into the dipping room

79
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:08,160
to get its sweet chocolate shell.

80
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:11,760
If the bacon's too warm, the chocolate won't set.

81
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:15,360
Too cold, and its fat juices will coagulate,

82
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:18,360
making the final product greasy.

83
00:04:18,361 --> 00:04:21,360
specific temperature conditions, too.

84
00:04:21,361 --> 00:04:23,360
This is a temperature-controlled room.

85
00:04:23,361 --> 00:04:27,360
We keep the chocolate at about 102 to 103 degrees at all time.

86
00:04:27,361 --> 00:04:29,360
Can't dip chocolate at that temperature.

87
00:04:29,361 --> 00:04:31,360
We need to get the chocolate down onto the marble surface,

88
00:04:31,361 --> 00:04:34,360
temper it, get it to room temperature.

89
00:04:34,361 --> 00:04:37,360
This heating and cooling process, called tempering,

90
00:04:37,361 --> 00:04:40,360
is the key to making the surface of the chocolate

91
00:04:40,361 --> 00:04:42,360
smooth and shiny.

92
00:04:42,361 --> 00:04:45,360
You try to dip with chocolate that's above room temperature.

93
00:04:45,361 --> 00:04:47,360
When it dries, it leaves a white film

94
00:04:47,361 --> 00:04:50,400
that definitely doesn't look very appetizing.

95
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:54,700
The bacon sits on drying racks for just 15 minutes.

96
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:58,100
Then it goes straight to the watering mouths of its fans.

97
00:04:58,200 --> 00:04:59,100
Oh, my God.

98
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:01,000
That's really good.

99
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:02,880
But bacon isn't the only unusual treat

100
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:06,020
the Marinis love to dip in chocolate.

101
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:08,720
We've tried dipping just about anything,

102
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:12,320
from insects to dried fruits to, I mean,

103
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:14,720
we'll put anything in chocolate and give it a taste.

104
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:16,160
But over the last four or five years,

105
00:05:16,161 --> 00:05:18,560
the salty and sweet has been pretty popular.

106
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:20,700
We take a ruffled salted potato chip

107
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:22,300
and dip that in chocolate.

108
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:24,360
You'd be surprised at the number of people we have come in

109
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:26,700
who buy the chips and the bacon at the same time.

110
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:27,900
Oh, it's delicious.

111
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,100
Thank you.

112
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:32,920
In increasing numbers,

113
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:35,120
candy makers are embracing the combination

114
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:38,320
of the sweet and the salty.

115
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:40,020
And with good reason.

116
00:05:40,120 --> 00:05:44,260
Our taste buds actually respond well to the mixture.

117
00:05:44,261 --> 00:05:47,700
You've got an average of 9,000 taste buds on your tongue

118
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:53,640
inside mushroom-shaped openings called fungiform papillae.

119
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:56,300
When you take a bite of chocolate-covered bacon,

120
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:58,980
its chemicals fall into the papillae,

121
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:01,980
where your taste buds translate the chemical signals

122
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:06,120
into electrical signals to your brain.

123
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:07,920
The sweet chocolate and salty bacon

124
00:06:08,020 --> 00:06:12,680
combine to send an unusually savory message.

125
00:06:12,681 --> 00:06:15,720
All of the nerves that are sending messages to the brain

126
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:17,220
actually work together.

127
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:21,420
So one receptor will enhance the function of another.

128
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:24,520
The receptor that's involved with sweet kind of makes the

129
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:27,520
nerves that are sensitive to salt a little more active.

130
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:31,340
The end result is that the two go very well together.

131
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:37,740
One candy maker's sweet and salty recipes

132
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:42,240
have attracted the taste buds of America's number one couple,

133
00:06:42,241 --> 00:06:46,740
President Barack Obama and his First Lady Michelle.

134
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:50,240
Meet celebrated confectioner Fran Bigelow.

135
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:53,480
His particular favorite was the smoked sea salt caramel,

136
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:56,380
and then Michelle the gray sea salt caramel

137
00:06:56,480 --> 00:06:57,580
and the dark chocolate.

138
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:00,200
We continue to supply the Oval Office with chocolates.

139
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:02,500
We've made a presidential box for them,

140
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:03,600
and they'll hand that out.

141
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:07,400
It's just, it's been very exciting.

142
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:10,040
Fran's Chocolates in Seattle, Washington,

143
00:07:10,041 --> 00:07:13,340
handcrafts chocolate-covered caramels sprinkled with sea

144
00:07:13,341 --> 00:07:16,540
salt.

145
00:07:16,541 --> 00:07:20,040
To perfect her sweet and salty specialty, Fran spent years

146
00:07:20,041 --> 00:07:23,180
searching for varieties of salt with just the right flavor

147
00:07:23,181 --> 00:07:25,280
profile.

148
00:07:25,281 --> 00:07:29,400
She discovered that gray sea salt, harvested off the coast of

149
00:07:29,401 --> 00:07:32,340
Brittany in France, complemented dark chocolate

150
00:07:32,341 --> 00:07:33,940
impeccably.

151
00:07:33,941 --> 00:07:37,540
And oak smoked salt from Wales is a perfect partner for milk

152
00:07:37,541 --> 00:07:39,740
chocolate.

153
00:07:39,741 --> 00:07:42,740
The smoked sea salt is softer and sweeter,

154
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:44,880
so it doesn't overpower the milk chocolate.

155
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:48,480
It's sort of seamless and just a wonderful combination with it.

156
00:07:48,580 --> 00:07:50,280
With the dark chocolate, we need something

157
00:07:50,380 --> 00:07:53,980
just a little more assertive, so it perks up your taste buds

158
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:55,880
and stands up to that caramel.

159
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:01,900
At her family-run kitchen facility,

160
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:07,100
Fran makes between 14,000 and 18,000 caramels a day.

161
00:08:07,101 --> 00:08:09,600
That's about 325 pounds.

162
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:17,940
Like many confections, caramel is made primarily of sugar.

163
00:08:18,040 --> 00:08:21,840
And step one is to melt it.

164
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:25,860
It all starts by heating 30 pounds of organic raw sugar

165
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:28,160
in a copper pot.

166
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:33,060
We use copper because it conducts heat so well.

167
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:35,000
Trying to completely melt the sugar

168
00:08:35,001 --> 00:08:38,240
so it doesn't have any other sugar crystals left in it.

169
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:41,240
So we will stay here until it is completely melted

170
00:08:41,340 --> 00:08:44,500
and let it just get really, really dark, caramely,

171
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:47,640
almost burnt to give it that wonderful, wonderful flavor.

172
00:08:52,020 --> 00:08:54,260
When the sugar is completely melted,

173
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:56,960
the caramel makers pour it into a 20-pound mixture

174
00:08:57,060 --> 00:09:00,560
of caramel's other key ingredients,

175
00:09:00,561 --> 00:09:04,800
cream, lemon juice, and butter.

176
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:08,900
The concoction then cooks for 45 minutes.

177
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,500
You don't want the caramel ever to lose the boil.

178
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:13,400
See, as you're adding ingredients to it,

179
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:16,080
because we're trying to also evaporate the moisture

180
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:19,780
out of this caramel to get it to a point where you can pick it up

181
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:21,420
and you can dip it in chocolate.

182
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:27,420
The boiling process removes virtually all the water

183
00:09:27,421 --> 00:09:32,920
mixture, resulting in a syrupy form of the chewy confection.

184
00:09:33,020 --> 00:09:35,020
It's then poured into custom molds.

185
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,060
After setting for 24 hours, the finished caramels are ready

186
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:44,000
to take a ride under a chocolate waterfall.

187
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:51,040
Franz chocolate, just like that of the Marini brothers,

188
00:09:51,140 --> 00:09:54,440
has undergone the tempering process that ensures a flawless

189
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:57,420
shell that tastes as good as it looks.

190
00:09:57,421 --> 00:10:00,660
The chocolate has been heated and then cooled

191
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:03,660
so that the cocoa butter crystals are all in alignment

192
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:08,540
and that they will provide you with the perfect texture

193
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:09,440
in your mouth.

194
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,940
As scrumptious as these chocolate caramels

195
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:17,140
are at this point, it's the sprinkle of salt

196
00:10:17,240 --> 00:10:20,140
that sets them apart.

197
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:23,880
What we found is once we added that little bit of sprinkle

198
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:27,420
of salt on the top, it just opened up the flavor.

199
00:10:27,421 --> 00:10:30,920
in your mouth, and we went, wow, this is it.

200
00:10:30,921 --> 00:10:33,320
Oh, my God.

201
00:10:33,321 --> 00:10:37,020
That's the wow that tickles the taste buds of the Obamas

202
00:10:37,021 --> 00:10:42,060
and countless other fans of Fran's caramels.

203
00:10:42,061 --> 00:10:43,860
Well, I got the advice from a friend of mine

204
00:10:43,861 --> 00:10:47,380
to always eat it upside down so you get the salt mixed first,

205
00:10:47,381 --> 00:10:48,480
and that's, like, a good tip.

206
00:10:48,481 --> 00:10:51,380
And ever since then, it's a really wonderful combination.

207
00:10:57,420 --> 00:11:01,220
Another type of candy not only satisfies your sweet tooth,

208
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:04,360
but also freshens your breath.

209
00:11:04,460 --> 00:11:05,360
Or does it?

210
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:12,060
On average, every American eats nine pounds of chocolate

211
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:13,980
per year.

212
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:17,940
That adds up to nearly 2.8 billion pounds,

213
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:20,280
almost half of the global consumption.

214
00:11:20,281 --> 00:11:27,280
consumption. This factory in Chicago churns

215
00:11:27,281 --> 00:11:32,520
out the number one selling hard candy in the United States.

216
00:11:32,521 --> 00:11:37,040
Any guesses what it is? Here's a hint.

217
00:11:37,041 --> 00:11:40,340
You'll encounter it most often on top of your restaurant check

218
00:11:40,341 --> 00:11:45,480
or your hotel pillow. Say hello to the peppermint

219
00:11:45,481 --> 00:11:49,240
starlight mint made by Primrose Candy.

220
00:11:49,241 --> 00:11:52,940
Every year, Americans pop 36 million of these iconic favorites

221
00:11:52,941 --> 00:11:56,940
into their mouths for a sweet rush and fresher breath.

222
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:02,740
That fresh feeling comes from peppermint's main active ingredient, menthol.

223
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:06,900
Menthol, which is in peppermint, is a very interesting compound

224
00:12:06,901 --> 00:12:08,880
because it has two effects.

225
00:12:09,060 --> 00:12:13,200
One is obviously to clean up and make your mouth feel fresh after the meal.

226
00:12:13,540 --> 00:12:17,940
But the other thing is it actually acts on the GI tract, on your gut,

227
00:12:17,941 --> 00:12:20,640
to move things along so your stomach empties

228
00:12:20,641 --> 00:12:22,740
a little more quickly, and so you don't feel

229
00:12:22,741 --> 00:12:24,540
quite so full and uncomfortable.

230
00:12:27,780 --> 00:12:30,380
The first step in the recipe is to cook a mixture

231
00:12:30,381 --> 00:12:32,180
of sugar and corn syrup.

232
00:12:34,180 --> 00:12:37,100
We cook it at 300 degrees to get all that water out.

233
00:12:37,101 --> 00:12:39,800
Now, it looks like it's actually very fluid

234
00:12:39,801 --> 00:12:42,540
at this point in time, like there is water in it.

235
00:12:42,541 --> 00:12:45,640
But right now, that is 98% solid.

236
00:12:45,641 --> 00:12:50,840
And the only reason why it's fluid is because it's at 260 degrees.

237
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:57,780
After the mixture is fully cooked, workers add the all-important peppermint.

238
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:02,160
It's so potent that all it takes is a couple of teaspoons,

239
00:13:03,740 --> 00:13:07,420
representing just two-tenths of one percent of the final product.

240
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:12,700
We use a natural peppermint oil that actually adds the mint flavor.

241
00:13:12,701 --> 00:13:16,300
The sugar and the corn syrup add the sweetness to it,

242
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:18,100
which makes it what you really like,

243
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:20,740
because peppermint oil is way too strong

244
00:13:20,840 --> 00:13:24,740
to have without the candy base.

245
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:27,640
After the liquidy syrup cooks for five minutes,

246
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:29,760
workers pour it onto a cooling table.

247
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:36,060
Within 10 minutes, this 140-pound mass of goo

248
00:13:36,160 --> 00:13:40,960
will be transformed into 10,000 finished mints.

249
00:13:40,961 --> 00:13:44,760
This table has chilled water running through it.

250
00:13:44,860 --> 00:13:48,760
The chilled water then keeps the surface cool,

251
00:13:48,860 --> 00:13:50,500
and it will cool the candy.

252
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:54,600
The candy right now, as you see, is far too lucid

253
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:56,600
to be put in the machine.

254
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:00,620
It needs to be in a plastic stain.

255
00:14:00,720 --> 00:14:03,420
As soon as the candy firms up a bit,

256
00:14:03,520 --> 00:14:08,560
mechanical arms need it into a taffy-like consistency.

257
00:14:08,561 --> 00:14:13,560
Then workers transfer the gooey mass to the molding table.

258
00:14:13,561 --> 00:14:16,560
Here the real fun begins.

259
00:14:16,561 --> 00:14:19,560
A red batch of candy cooked and mixed on the other side of the

260
00:14:19,561 --> 00:14:24,560
kitchen joins the white candy mass.

261
00:14:24,561 --> 00:14:28,560
Then, like kids playing with Play-Doh, they roll both red and

262
00:14:28,561 --> 00:14:31,560
white sections into long strips.

263
00:14:31,561 --> 00:14:34,560
Right now they're making the stripes for the starlight mint.

264
00:14:34,561 --> 00:14:35,261
mint.

265
00:14:35,262 --> 00:14:40,800
What we did is we took one stripe, stretched it out the length of the table, and then cut it in

266
00:14:40,801 --> 00:14:42,479
half to make two stripes.

267
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:47,180
Then we took the two stripes, stretched it out the length of the table

268
00:14:47,181 --> 00:14:48,431
to make four stripes.

269
00:14:48,432 --> 00:14:52,720
Then when we got the four stripes, we stretched it out the length of the

270
00:14:52,721 --> 00:14:55,716
table and cut it in threes to make it twelve stripes.

271
00:14:55,717 --> 00:14:57,920
The twelve stripes is the jacket, which

272
00:14:57,921 --> 00:14:59,413
he's now putting together.

273
00:14:59,414 --> 00:15:03,260
The jacket basically makes up about thirty percent of the starlight

274
00:15:03,261 --> 00:15:07,100
and the center makes up 70%.

275
00:15:07,101 --> 00:15:10,900
And then we're going to roll the body into the jacket

276
00:15:10,901 --> 00:15:14,500
and make one big, massive, almost like a candy cane

277
00:15:14,501 --> 00:15:15,600
without the bench.

278
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:20,820
The trick now is to thin this five-foot-long,

279
00:15:20,821 --> 00:15:23,920
two-foot-wide candy burrito into a strip

280
00:15:23,921 --> 00:15:25,920
with a diameter of less than an inch.

281
00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:31,920
An extruding machine handles that challenge.

282
00:15:31,921 --> 00:15:36,720
challenge, rolling and stretching the mass down to a width the size

283
00:15:36,721 --> 00:15:37,620
of the final mint.

284
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:46,360
Finally, razor sharp blades form the finished mints,

285
00:15:46,361 --> 00:15:47,660
110 at a time.

286
00:15:50,980 --> 00:15:53,700
They taste as good as they look, but are they really

287
00:15:53,701 --> 00:15:55,380
any match for chronic bad breath?

288
00:15:58,080 --> 00:15:59,920
It's actually a mask.

289
00:15:59,921 --> 00:16:03,720
These mints, the menthol in it, volatilizes very easily.

290
00:16:03,721 --> 00:16:07,720
What I mean by volatilize is that it becomes like a gas.

291
00:16:07,721 --> 00:16:11,760
In other words, the molecules, the menthol spreads in the air,

292
00:16:11,761 --> 00:16:16,560
and you have the impression that you have actually cleaned up

293
00:16:16,561 --> 00:16:20,680
your bad smells here.

294
00:16:20,681 --> 00:16:23,680
Experts say the most effective countermeasure to bad breath

295
00:16:23,681 --> 00:16:25,920
is proper dental hygiene.

296
00:16:25,921 --> 00:16:28,880
But mints can augment good brushing and flossing habits

297
00:16:28,881 --> 00:16:35,880
fix when we need it.

298
00:16:35,881 --> 00:16:38,880
Some candies combine that quick fix with a sweet taste of

299
00:16:38,881 --> 00:16:42,880
chocolate, like the number one selling after-dinner mint,

300
00:16:42,881 --> 00:16:45,880
Andy's Creme de Mente.

301
00:16:45,881 --> 00:16:48,880
10 million of the three-layered green foil-wrapped candies are

302
00:16:48,881 --> 00:16:56,880
made every day at this factory in Delavan, Wisconsin.

303
00:16:56,881 --> 00:17:00,080
a surprising backstory to the familiar Mountain logo

304
00:17:00,081 --> 00:17:04,480
and the mint's name. It dates back to 1920,

305
00:17:04,481 --> 00:17:09,320
and the candy maker that created it, Andrew Canellos.

306
00:17:09,321 --> 00:17:13,320
At first, these box chocolates were called Andes

307
00:17:13,321 --> 00:17:17,440
because the name of the inventor was Andrew.

308
00:17:17,441 --> 00:17:20,440
But some people wouldn't like to give their girlfriends

309
00:17:20,441 --> 00:17:25,080
or their wives a box of chocolates with another man's name.

310
00:17:25,081 --> 00:17:27,840
And so they came up with the name Andes,

311
00:17:27,940 --> 00:17:32,320
but spelt it A-N-D-E-S after the Andes Mountains

312
00:17:32,420 --> 00:17:33,420
in South America.

313
00:17:37,820 --> 00:17:40,220
Many details of the Andes production process

314
00:17:40,320 --> 00:17:44,040
have been tightly guarded secrets for decades,

315
00:17:44,140 --> 00:17:48,040
secrets now held by Tootsie Roll Industries.

316
00:17:48,140 --> 00:17:51,380
The recipe for the Andes crimp de mentheens

317
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:54,740
has remained the same since they were first introduced

318
00:17:54,741 --> 00:17:58,680
in 1950, and it is top secret.

319
00:18:03,380 --> 00:18:07,580
Even so, Andy's clued us in on a few facts.

320
00:18:07,680 --> 00:18:09,080
The chocolate portion of the candy

321
00:18:09,180 --> 00:18:12,020
is made by machines that grind cocoa powder and sugar

322
00:18:12,120 --> 00:18:17,060
against each other to create an extremely fine mixture.

323
00:18:17,160 --> 00:18:20,840
Each particle is only 26 microns in diameter,

324
00:18:20,940 --> 00:18:22,400
or about 1,000th of an inch.

325
00:18:24,740 --> 00:18:29,740
That's about half the width of a single human hair.

326
00:18:29,741 --> 00:18:32,740
This is the powdered product that comes off

327
00:18:32,741 --> 00:18:37,740
of the finished refiner, and we want this product to be this fine

328
00:18:37,741 --> 00:18:41,740
so that you have that smooth chocolate feel in your mouth.

329
00:18:41,741 --> 00:18:45,740
So we taste it to determine if it meets our flavor profile,

330
00:18:45,741 --> 00:18:49,740
and we rub it on our bottom lip as a double check

331
00:18:49,741 --> 00:18:52,740
for the particle size reduction to make sure that it's as smooth

332
00:18:52,741 --> 00:18:56,580
as we expect it to be at this point.

333
00:18:56,680 --> 00:18:58,380
Conveyors carry the fine granules

334
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:01,380
to a large blender called a conch.

335
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:04,480
Oil and butter are added, and the conch mixes them

336
00:19:04,580 --> 00:19:07,180
for five hours.

337
00:19:07,280 --> 00:19:09,500
The result is liquid chocolate.

338
00:19:14,300 --> 00:19:16,240
It's the mint portion of the candy, however,

339
00:19:16,340 --> 00:19:20,100
that makes Andes such a popular favorite.

340
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:22,740
But how it becomes part of the finished product

341
00:19:22,741 --> 00:19:25,740
is the big secret its makers keep safely locked away.

342
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:31,740
All we can say is that it consists of a blend of peppermint

343
00:19:31,741 --> 00:19:35,480
oils that gives each triple-layered candy its signature

344
00:19:35,481 --> 00:19:36,380
fresh kick.

345
00:19:39,900 --> 00:19:43,200
That also applies for a variation of the original candy,

346
00:19:43,201 --> 00:19:47,100
called Andy's Creme de Mente Parfait, which inverts the green

347
00:19:47,101 --> 00:19:49,900
portion that's normally in the middle into the two outside

348
00:19:49,901 --> 00:19:50,601
layers.

349
00:19:52,740 --> 00:19:59,740
Andy's isn't the only popular candy that has a name with a

350
00:19:59,741 --> 00:20:05,740
colorful history, and it's at the center of an unsolved mystery.

351
00:20:05,741 --> 00:20:09,740
Who exactly were the enigmatic Mike and Ike?

352
00:20:09,741 --> 00:20:15,740
Peppermint was the original flavor of Pez, invented by an

353
00:20:15,741 --> 00:20:19,740
Austrian candy maker in 1927. Derived from the German word for

354
00:20:19,741 --> 00:20:22,040
Derived from the German word for peppermint,

355
00:20:22,041 --> 00:20:26,380
it utilizes the first, middle, and last letters of peppermints.

356
00:20:29,180 --> 00:20:32,280
Going to the movies just isn't the same without something sweet

357
00:20:32,281 --> 00:20:35,280
to munch on. And your local Cineplex

358
00:20:35,281 --> 00:20:39,100
couldn't survive if you weren't hooked.

359
00:20:39,101 --> 00:20:42,000
Candy and concession sales are the lifeblood of the theater

360
00:20:42,001 --> 00:20:44,800
business, accounting for 40% of profits.

361
00:20:44,801 --> 00:20:49,300
secrets.

362
00:20:49,301 --> 00:20:53,940
One candy contributing to that windfall is Dots, the number one

363
00:20:53,941 --> 00:20:57,940
selling gumdrop brand in the United States.

364
00:20:57,941 --> 00:21:03,080
These colorful treats have plenty of colorful secrets,

365
00:21:03,081 --> 00:21:05,920
starting with the enormous facility where they're made.

366
00:21:08,660 --> 00:21:12,120
The building was originally built during World War II,

367
00:21:12,121 --> 00:21:17,020
And it was built to manufacture Chrysler engines for B-29 bombers.

368
00:21:19,920 --> 00:21:22,620
Later, Maverick automaker Preston Tucker

369
00:21:22,621 --> 00:21:27,220
took over the factory, producing the 50 cars bearing his name

370
00:21:27,221 --> 00:21:29,220
that became prized collector's items.

371
00:21:32,020 --> 00:21:35,880
Now, it's dots rolling off the assembly line here,

372
00:21:35,881 --> 00:21:39,320
a staggering 24 million individual pieces every day.

373
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:46,120
They're made from 136,000 pounds of gooey sweetness

374
00:21:46,220 --> 00:21:49,120
that's cooked 600 gallons at a time.

375
00:21:49,220 --> 00:21:53,860
Dots are made by mixing ingredients of sugar, corn syrup,

376
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:55,660
water, and starch.

377
00:21:55,760 --> 00:21:58,160
It makes a 6,000-pound batch of dots.

378
00:21:58,260 --> 00:22:02,980
And we make about 30,000 pounds of dots a day.

379
00:22:03,080 --> 00:22:05,580
Starch is what gives dots their characteristic

380
00:22:05,680 --> 00:22:09,620
thick, gummy texture.

381
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:12,120
When it's finished cooking, along with all the other ingredients,

382
00:22:12,121 --> 00:22:16,920
the mixture flows into six tanks that produce six distinct colors

383
00:22:17,020 --> 00:22:20,120
and flavors simultaneously.

384
00:22:20,220 --> 00:22:23,760
Workers add one liquid containing an artificial color,

385
00:22:23,860 --> 00:22:26,760
a second an artificial flavor.

386
00:22:26,860 --> 00:22:29,240
They're meticulous about matching their combos,

387
00:22:29,340 --> 00:22:32,740
so your yellow dot tastes like a lemon and not a cherry.

388
00:22:36,940 --> 00:22:38,720
The challenge of forming the dot's shape

389
00:22:38,820 --> 00:22:40,940
starts with this machine.

390
00:22:40,941 --> 00:22:42,940
The mogul.

391
00:22:42,941 --> 00:22:46,940
It makes a custom mold by filling these trays with starch,

392
00:22:46,941 --> 00:22:49,940
the same substance that's in the dots themselves.

393
00:22:49,941 --> 00:22:53,940
And dyes come down and make holes in the starch,

394
00:22:53,941 --> 00:22:57,940
and then the liquid formula is poured into these holes

395
00:22:57,941 --> 00:23:00,940
to form the shape of the dots.

396
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:06,940
This starch mold, unlike one made from metal,

397
00:23:06,941 --> 00:23:08,940
prevents the candy from sticking

398
00:23:08,941 --> 00:23:11,940
and enables easier removal and handling.

399
00:23:11,941 --> 00:23:14,940
28 trays are filled every minute,

400
00:23:14,941 --> 00:23:18,940
creating more than 1.7 million dots every hour.

401
00:23:20,940 --> 00:23:22,940
The starch mold is still hot at this point

402
00:23:22,941 --> 00:23:24,940
and about 24% moisture.

403
00:23:24,941 --> 00:23:27,940
The dots are very liquid and still hot and oozy.

404
00:23:27,941 --> 00:23:30,940
You actually pick them up and you can smear them,

405
00:23:30,941 --> 00:23:33,940
and eventually we dry this down.

406
00:23:33,941 --> 00:23:37,940
But right now it's a very liquid molten material.

407
00:23:38,940 --> 00:23:42,780
The dots dry over the next 24 hours

408
00:23:42,781 --> 00:23:44,980
as the starch mold absorbs moisture,

409
00:23:44,981 --> 00:23:47,580
ensuring the desired final texture.

410
00:23:47,581 --> 00:23:49,280
After that curing time,

411
00:23:49,281 --> 00:23:51,280
we actually turn these trays over,

412
00:23:51,281 --> 00:23:53,780
and these dots go on for further processing.

413
00:23:56,300 --> 00:23:57,700
And the dots head off

414
00:23:57,701 --> 00:24:01,900
for their final treatment, polishing.

415
00:24:01,901 --> 00:24:03,700
That's the job of this machine.

416
00:24:03,701 --> 00:24:06,600
It looks like it was designed by Willy Wonka,

417
00:24:06,601 --> 00:24:11,600
called the Z Blender. The dots, when they go into the

418
00:24:11,601 --> 00:24:15,600
Z Blender, have a little bit of starch residue on them, which

419
00:24:15,601 --> 00:24:18,600
makes them a little bit dull. And the Z Blender removes the

420
00:24:18,601 --> 00:24:22,600
starch and puts a shine on them, and the shine is what gives

421
00:24:22,601 --> 00:24:27,600
them the fresh dot appearance. As popular as dots are

422
00:24:27,601 --> 00:24:31,600
with moviegoers, they're not the only chewy candies buying for

423
00:24:31,601 --> 00:24:35,600
top billing. Mike and Ike has been a blockbuster

424
00:24:35,601 --> 00:24:39,600
hit with candy lovers since 1940.

425
00:24:39,601 --> 00:24:42,600
The sweet with a chewy center and a hard candy shell

426
00:24:42,601 --> 00:24:47,600
was the brainchild of Just Born, a family-owned company owned

427
00:24:47,601 --> 00:24:50,600
by Russian immigrant Sam Born.

428
00:24:50,601 --> 00:24:52,600
He opened up a little shop in Brooklyn, New York,

429
00:24:52,601 --> 00:24:54,600
and used to put a sign in a window that read,

430
00:24:54,601 --> 00:24:57,600
the candy's so fresh it's as if it was just born that day.

431
00:24:57,601 --> 00:25:00,600
And it was a play on the name, and the name stuck.

432
00:25:02,600 --> 00:25:04,600
But the story of how Mike and Ike Candy got its name

433
00:25:04,601 --> 00:25:10,600
got its name is one of the candy world's great unsolved mysteries.

434
00:25:10,601 --> 00:25:13,100
Who were Mike and Ike?

435
00:25:13,101 --> 00:25:15,300
Don't ask the folks who just born.

436
00:25:15,301 --> 00:25:17,600
Even they don't know.

437
00:25:17,601 --> 00:25:20,600
There's a lot of mystery surrounding how the name Mike and Ike came to be.

438
00:25:20,601 --> 00:25:22,600
We do know a couple of things.

439
00:25:22,601 --> 00:25:25,100
Mike and Ike were popular names back then.

440
00:25:25,101 --> 00:25:27,300
There was President Ike Eisenhower.

441
00:25:27,301 --> 00:25:30,100
There was a vaudeville act named Mike and Ike.

442
00:25:30,101 --> 00:25:34,100
And we think for all of these reasons, somehow the name Mike and Ike came to be.

443
00:25:34,600 --> 00:25:37,600
Today's fans of Mike and Ike care a lot more about what the

444
00:25:37,700 --> 00:25:40,600
candy tastes like than the origins of its name.

445
00:25:40,700 --> 00:25:42,600
Mr. Candy.

446
00:25:42,700 --> 00:25:47,140
At its factory in Bethlehem, Pennsylvania,

447
00:25:47,240 --> 00:25:50,840
Just Born produces as many as 70 million individual pieces

448
00:25:50,940 --> 00:25:52,460
of Mike and Ike per day.

449
00:25:56,060 --> 00:26:00,060
That's a mind-blowing $25 billion a year,

450
00:26:00,160 --> 00:26:04,600
or enough to fill 500 Olympic-sized swimming pools.

451
00:26:04,601 --> 00:26:09,600
Not surprisingly, the main ingredients for Mike and Ike's

452
00:26:09,700 --> 00:26:13,240
are the same as those for many other candies.

453
00:26:13,340 --> 00:26:17,140
Sugar, corn syrup, and starch.

454
00:26:17,240 --> 00:26:19,840
And the familiar Mogul machine makes starch molds

455
00:26:19,940 --> 00:26:21,960
that form the chewy centers.

456
00:26:22,060 --> 00:26:24,060
Once the centers come out of the Mogul,

457
00:26:24,160 --> 00:26:26,460
they come up to this area where they're cooled.

458
00:26:26,560 --> 00:26:29,400
Once cooled, they'll go into trays like you see beside me.

459
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:31,660
At this point, the center is flavorful,

460
00:26:31,661 --> 00:26:32,660
beside me.

461
00:26:32,760 --> 00:26:34,960
At this point, the center is flavorless and ready to go

462
00:26:35,060 --> 00:26:39,060
to the flavoring area, but it still tastes pretty good.

463
00:26:39,160 --> 00:26:39,860
It's very sweet.

464
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:47,000
Here's the place where Mike and Ikes find their flavor,

465
00:26:47,100 --> 00:26:49,020
at a station that looks more like a laundromat

466
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:50,120
than a candy factory.

467
00:26:54,520 --> 00:26:59,560
The rotating containers perform a process called cold panning.

468
00:26:59,561 --> 00:27:03,860
Inside each container are the flavorless chewy centers,

469
00:27:03,960 --> 00:27:07,700
to which workers add sugar, syrup, and the all-important

470
00:27:07,800 --> 00:27:12,300
color and flavors. The pan's rotating motion

471
00:27:12,400 --> 00:27:15,200
causes a colorful sugary shell to accumulate

472
00:27:15,300 --> 00:27:18,620
around each soft center.

473
00:27:18,720 --> 00:27:21,620
They'll go through a multi-step process of adding

474
00:27:21,720 --> 00:27:25,460
various ingredients to build that flavor coating onto the

475
00:27:25,461 --> 00:27:26,860
to the Mike and Ike Center.

476
00:27:26,861 --> 00:27:30,660
It's not even a science per se, it's an art form,

477
00:27:30,661 --> 00:27:32,900
and it's something that not everybody can do.

478
00:27:32,901 --> 00:27:36,100
It's watching the product as it's being flavored

479
00:27:36,101 --> 00:27:37,980
so that they can make adjustments,

480
00:27:37,981 --> 00:27:40,440
add different ingredients at different times,

481
00:27:40,441 --> 00:27:42,740
depending on how the product is reacting.

482
00:27:45,220 --> 00:27:47,420
The flavors added during this artful process

483
00:27:47,421 --> 00:27:51,120
range from newer entries like Caribbean Punch

484
00:27:51,121 --> 00:27:55,220
to traditional standbys like Cherry, Lemon,

485
00:27:55,221 --> 00:27:57,880
lime, and orange.

486
00:27:57,881 --> 00:28:00,060
Orange, Mike and Ike, the original.

487
00:28:03,820 --> 00:28:06,660
Each piece is polished with carnauba wax,

488
00:28:06,661 --> 00:28:10,060
derived from the leaves of Brazilian palm trees.

489
00:28:10,061 --> 00:28:11,980
The wax is commonly used in the candy industry

490
00:28:11,981 --> 00:28:17,580
to polish all sorts of treats and as a coating for dental floss.

491
00:28:17,581 --> 00:28:19,620
Now, at this point, you can see that the candy still

492
00:28:19,621 --> 00:28:21,580
has a powdery finish to it.

493
00:28:21,581 --> 00:28:24,720
So what we do behind me is we polish the candy.

494
00:28:24,721 --> 00:28:27,860
The polishing not only gives the center a nice, shiny look,

495
00:28:28,100 --> 00:28:29,860
but it also helps to protect the candy.

496
00:28:30,120 --> 00:28:33,060
It keeps moisture from getting into the candy to make it soft,

497
00:28:33,320 --> 00:28:35,640
and it keeps moisture from getting out of the candy

498
00:28:35,641 --> 00:28:36,640
to make it hard.

499
00:28:39,040 --> 00:28:41,560
The polished product sets for four hours

500
00:28:41,561 --> 00:28:42,740
before heading to packaging.

501
00:28:45,340 --> 00:28:47,500
And the candy with the mysterious name

502
00:28:47,501 --> 00:28:49,380
is ready for the movie theater.

503
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:53,980
There's no mystery about the name of this popular candy,

504
00:28:53,981 --> 00:28:58,980
But few know the identity of the ingredient

505
00:28:59,080 --> 00:29:01,980
that gives it its super sour punch.

506
00:29:06,020 --> 00:29:08,420
As popular as candy is at the movies,

507
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:10,740
it still ranks behind popcorn.

508
00:29:10,840 --> 00:29:12,540
But it's the better value.

509
00:29:12,640 --> 00:29:16,640
A theater's average profit margin on popcorn is 86%.

